title: 'statement-store: skip naturally-expired hashes from expired map'
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    ## Problem

    Implement #11665

    The statement store maintains an `expired: HashMap<Hash, u64>` to suppress
    re-gossip of removed statements. It grew unboundedly because:

    1. **All** removed statements were inserted regardless of removal reason.
       Statements removed because their own expiry timestamp had already passed
       were included even though `submit` rejects them unconditionally via
       `AlreadyExpired` — the map was never consulted for them.

    2. The purge (`maintain`) used `HashMap::retain`, which is O(n) over the
       entire map. With `purge_after_sec = 48h` and high eviction rates the map
       could reach ~100M entries / ~9GB RAM, with a full scan every 29 seconds.

    ## Changes

    `make_expired` now checks `current_time < expiry.get_expiration_timestamp_secs()`
    before inserting into the expired map. Statements whose own timestamp has already
    passed are skipped in both the in-memory map and `col::EXPIRED` on disk.

    The three DB-write sites (`submit`, `remove`, `remove_by`) gate the `col::EXPIRED`
    write on `index.expired.contains(&hash)`, matching the in-memory decision.

    O(log n + k) purge via EvictedIndex

    Added `Expiry::get_expiration_timestamp_secs()`
crates:
- name: sc-statement-store
  bump: patch
